[68.162556, "o", "\u001b[?25l\u001b[?2026h\u001b[?7l\u001b[28;1H\u001b[0m\u001b[K\u001b[48;2;15;18;22;39m \u001b[38;2;229;88;196m⠧ 4s\u001b[39m \u001b[38;2;42;48;56m>\u001b[39m \u001b[38;2;229;88;196m◔ Sonnet 4.6\u001b[39m \u001b[38;2;42;48;56m>\u001b[39m \u001b[38;2;232;236;244m🗑 omp-agenda-demo\u001b[39m \u001b[38;2;42;48;56m>\u001b[39m \u001b[38;2;212;192;144mS0.11\u001b[39m \u001b[0;38;2;15;18;22m▶\u001b[0;49;38;2;229;88;196m─1%\u001b[305.386459, "o", "\u001b[?25l\u001b[?2026h\u001b[?7l\u001b[1;1H\u001b[2K\u001b[2;1H\u001b[J\u001b[1;1H\u001b[1;1H                                                                                                              \u001b[0m\r\r\n Options: API-layer only (Redis behind your service), CDN in front (e.g. CloudFront/Fastly caching HTTP       \u001b[0m\r\r\n responses), or both as a multi-tier?                                                                         \u001b[0m\r\r\n                                                                                                              \u001b[0m\r\r\n\u001b[0m\u001b[K \u001b[1mMy lean: API-layer (server-side) first.\u001b[22m Your write-invalidation strategy is much easier to control there —   \u001b[0m\r\r\n CDN adds complexity around purge APIs, auth'd endpoints usually can't be CDN-cached anyway, and you already  \u001b[0m\r\r\n have Redis in place. Add CDN only for public, read-heavy, slow-changing resources if you need it later.      \u001b[0m\r\r\n                                                                                                              \u001b[0m\r\r\n\u001b[0m\u001b[K What does your API surface look like — mostly authenticated per-user data, public data, or mixed?            \u001b[0m\r\r\n\u001b[0m\u001b[K\r\r\n\u001b]133;A\u0007\u001b[48;2;15;18;22m                                                                                                              \u001b[49m\u001b[0m\r\r\n\u001b[48;2;15;18;22m \u001b[38;2;229;229;231mAgreed with your suggestion. Next.\u001b[39m                                                                           \u001b[49m\u001b[0m\r\r\n\u001b[48;2;15;18;22m                                                                                                              \u001b[49m\u001b]133;B\u0007\u001b]133;C\u0007\u001b]133;D;0\u0007\u001b[0m\r\r\n\u001b[0m\u001b[K\r\r\n\u001b[0m\u001b[K\u001b[39;1magenda\u001b[22;39m \u001b[38;2;156;163;176mupdate 2 decided → Server-side only (Redis behind API layer); CDN deferred\u001b[38;2;107;114;128m, \u001b[39mfocus 3\u001b[39m                    \u001b[0m\r\r\n\u001b[0m\u001b[K\r\r\n \u001b[1m[3] What's your eviction policy when Redis hits its memory limit?\u001b[22m                                            \u001b[0m\r\r\n                                                                                                              \u001b[0m\r\r\n Options: \u001b[38;2;0;255;136mallkeys-lru\u001b[39m (evict any key, least-recently-used), \u001b[38;2;0;255;136mvolatile-lru\u001b[39m (evict only keys with a TTL set), or \u001b[0m\r\r\n\u001b[0m\u001b[K \u001b[38;2;0;255;136mnoeviction\u001b[39m (reject writes — dangerous for a shared session+cache cluster).                                   \u001b[0m\r\r\n                                                                                                              \u001b[0m\r\r\n\u001b[0m\u001b[K \u001b[1mMy lean: \u001b[38;2;0;255;136mvolatile-lru\u001b[39m.\u001b[22m Since you're sharing Redis with sessions, you can't risk evicting session keys —      \u001b[0m\r\r\n \u001b[38;2;0;255;136mvolatile-lru\u001b[39m restricts eviction to TTL-tagged cache keys and leaves sessionless keys alone. Just make sure   \u001b[0m\r\r\n every cache key you write has a TTL.                                                                         \u001b[0m\r\r\n                                                                                                              \u001b[0m\r\r\n Are session keys stored with or without a TTL?                                                               \u001b[0m\r\r\n\u001b[0m\u001b[K\r\r\n\u001b[0m\u001b[K \u001b[38;2;107;114;128;3m※ recap: Designing your product API caching layer — just decided on Redis (server-side only, invalidate on   \u001b[0m\r\r\n \u001b[3;38;2;107;114;128mwrite) and working through eviction policy [3]. Next: you need to answer whether session keys in Redis are   \u001b[0m\r\r\n \u001b[3;38;2;107;114;128mstored with or without a TTL.\u001b[23;39m                                                                                \u001b[0m\r\r\n\u001b[0m\u001b[K\r\r\n                                                                        \u001b[2;3mCaching layer design interview session\u001b[23;22m\u001b[0m\r\r\n\u001b[0m\u001b[K\r\r\n\u001b[0m\u001b[K\u001b[48;2;15;18;22;38;2;0;180;255m▌\u001b[39m \u001b[48;2;0;130;179;38;2;0;180;255;1m Agenda \u001b[22;39;49;48;2;15;18;22m \u001b[48;2;0;130;179m \u001b[38;2;156;163;176m○\u001b[39;38;2;0;180;255;1m3\u001b[22;39m \u001b[49;48;2;15;18;22m \u001b[38;2;156;163;176m○\u001b[39;38;2;107;114;128m4\u001b[39m \u001b[38;2;156;163;176m○\u001b[39;38;2;107;114;128m5\u001b[39m \u001b[38;2;156;163;176m○\u001b[39;38;2;107;114;128m6\u001b[39m \u001b[38"]
